I've written the assessment to session_plan/assessment.md and committed it. The harness had already confirmed the build and tests pass. I didn't re-run the full suite; I only ran the binary directly.

New bug, seen in a real run: every social run reports that the process died before finishing (no_terminal_emit), even when it finished fine. In run 36463335955 (18:10 today, marked success), the report prints "no usage record — the process did not reach its terminal emit", and the very next line shows 20 usage records written for that run.
• **Cause:** `scripts/social.sh` looks for the `↳` cost line in the captured output. When my output is piped I switch to quiet mode, and quiet mode never prints that line, so the search can't match.
• **Fix:** read the cost from the usage record in .yoyo/audit.jsonl, the way daily_diary.sh already does.
• Status: I predicted this yesterday from reading the code; today is the first time it showed up in a real log. social.sh isn't a protected file, so I can fix it myself (it's part of #944).

My earlier fixes hold (checked by running the binary):
• --print outputs exactly PONG.
• Plain -p still passes the model's leading blank lines through. That's expected: the help text now says so.
• `yoyo todo add` at the shell refuses and exits with code 1 instead of printing a green ✓ and forgetting.

Creator changes today:
• **Wrap-up commits are now restricted** (`a3c0a7a3`). The end-of-session sweep may only commit `journals/`, `memory/`, `session_plan/` and `.yoyo/`. Code written anywhere else outside a task is refused and saved as a patch, not shipped.
• **Correction to my #951 report:** my example commit `6e79f686` never reached main. It was committed locally and the job was cancelled before pushing. The hole I reported was real; my example of it was wrong.
• CI now also runs extract_trajectory.py --test.

Research (from Claude Code's changelog):
• They fixed `claude -p losing an answer it had already produced when a turn dies mid-stream. My --print` now holds back all output until the final answer, so it may have the same problem. I haven't tested this yet.
• They added a machine-readable list of MCP servers that failed to start to headless JSON output.
• They shipped a --safe-mode that starts with all customisations off, the same thing #879 asks for.
• I skipped the yopedia recall and save step this session because I ran out of context once already.

Suggested priorities for the planner:
1. Fix the `social.sh spend report. The test must use a capture with no ↳` line, which is what a real run produces, not a stand-in that prints it.
2. Check whether --print loses a partly streamed answer, using the fake local server that yesterday's process-level test already runs against. A clean result is a valid outcome.
3. Take #936 (the 50 commands that still start a paid session from the shell) or #879 (composite safe mode).
